Ah, yes, how to play a Wizard.  I wanted to actually win with one
before holding forth on this subject, but since you asked...

My objectives when I play a Wizard in the early stages of the game are
1) to survive and 2) to get ready for the castle and later.  And just
that.  Wizards start off with wimpy strength, and often get worse when
you try to aquire poison resistance.  So when the going gets tough, I
only carry what I need to survive while I collect intrinsics.  I know I
can always go back to retrieve things I leave by the staircases once I
get strong.  But once a Wizard is Ready, he is Really Ready.

If I have a ring of poison resistance early on, I wear it, and keep it
on all the time.  And then I eat every poisonous critter I can kill.
If I don't have the ring, then I try to get P.R. from cave spiders
first, or else wait until I can kill a unicorn.  P.R. is easy to get
once you have the unicorn horn; just apply it every time eating a
poisonous critter hurts you.  Keep applying it until are stats are back
to max.

> 3) Is an athame an o.k. weapon or just a great BIC. Sting is easily
> aquired while other artifacts elude me. Since my god (neutral) isn't
> exactly Santa Claus, other ways of getting good weapons would be
> appreciated.

An athame is better than a large black knife.  Sting is better than an
athame against Orcs only.  Excalibur is normally easy enough to make,
although right now, Mandru the Magician has penetrated to level 25 and
has yet to see a long sword.  Get the spoilers and keep trading up on
weapons as opportunity allows.  My own favorite weapon, if I can make
it fairly early on in the game is a heavy iron ball named Skullcrusher
(not really an artifact, but effective, especially with good
dexterity).  I always trade up, though, if I can find a long sword to
make Excalibur or either a chaotic or neutral altar (every self
respecting Wizard takes a certain amount of professional pride in
fabricating his own artifact).

I save lawful altars for obvious reasons, and convert chaotic altars
(yes, I know what they are good for, but I don't choose to play it that
way-- just a matter of personal preferance).  Thoth is a source of two
highly underrated weapons, Fire Brand and Frost Brand.  I keep
sacrificing until I get Frost Brand, because I figure in hell, critters
are likely to be fire resistant.  Compare Frosty (as it is
affectionately known in The Trade) with Mjollnir, which is a big
favorite in this news group.  Mjollnir gives you from 1 to 4 points of
damage from being a war hammer and an extra 24 points shock damage
against large critters.  So a blessed +8 war hammer named Mjollnir
gives you 25 to 28 points of damage against large non-shock resistant
critters, with maybe an extra 1 to 3 points damage against demons and
the undead.  Meanwhile a blessed +8 longsword named Frost Brand gives
you 1 to 12 points damage from being a long sword, and then an
additional damage due to cold that comes from adding in again the
enchantment, the damage from being the long sword, and the damage from
the blessedness against demons/undead.  So your blessed +8 Frosty gives
you 10 to 40 points of damage against large non-cold-resistant
critters, plus maybe an extra 2 to 6 points damage against demons and
undead.  So remember: not just any brand-- Frost Brand.

To get ready to do the castle, I try to get a blessed rustproof +8
Frosty as my weapon of choice, and then Excalibur with which to fight
wraiths and vampires.  I'll enchant Excalibur as far as I can (up to
+8) *after* I get Frosty taken care of.  Although, given how things are
going in my current game, I don't intend to bother with Excalibur even
if I do find a long sword, because it's only a little further down to
the castle.  I already have a prepared Frosty, plus three scrolls of
enchant weapon (two of them already blessed).  So I figure to wish for
a blessed rustproof +2 broadsword named Stormbringer (I can do that on
my home-patched nethack), enchant it to +8, bless the last scroll of
enchant weapon, and take a risk at enchanting Frosty to +11.  If all
that works successfully, Mandru once again will be One Fly Mother.

